# motivation

this series focuses on an impact study when libnix integration takes off. the key assumption is that nix works on all major platforms (windows, linux, macos) and libnix is integrated into PMs like cargo.

see series [libnix](https://lastlog.de/blog/timeline.html?filter=series::libnix)

# impact

## source / binary downloads

if Cargo were routinely paired with libnix so that Hydra retained the intermediate build artifacts for foundational crates (syn, rand, libc, hashbrown, proc-macro2 and the rest of the usual dependency spine), almost every subsequent cargo build would resolve those crates from the shared binary cache instead of crates.io. download volume on crates.io for those packages would collapse toward zero while the load on hydra.nixos.org would rise sharply, turning the NixOS continuous-build infrastructure into the de-facto global cache for the majority of Rust compilations.


* cargo, like nix, is also source deployment but it mainly uses a local cache, the famous ./target folder.
* a library like https://crates.io/crates/hashbrown which has about 8M downloads/day will drop to zero when using libnix in cargo as it will most likely been built by hydra at some point.

## build times

* when using a cargo/rustc version currently in use on nixpkgs, expect most dependencies to be just binary substitutes instead of source downloads and source builds.
* when using bleeding edge / specialized settings of cargo/rustc, expect a +30% overall build time because of the build isolation coming with nix

the biggest impact on build time will be the current cargo handling of build.rs which currently comes with lax requirements on dependency outlining causing lots of rebuilds, see https://github.com/nixcloud/cargo/issues/3


